<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/"
      type="topic" id="canvas">
  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#design"/>
    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <link type="seealso" xref="view"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="properties-panel"/>
    <desc>Select, move, resize, group, align and stack the elements on a
    label, and undo what you did.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Select, move and arrange elements</title>

  <section id="select">
    <title>Select</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>Click</title>
        <p>Selects the topmost element under the pointer. A click on empty
        canvas selects nothing.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><keyseq><key>Shift</key></keyseq>-click</title>
        <p>Adds an element to the selection, or takes it out again.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Drag on empty canvas</title>
        <p>Draws a band. Everything the band touches is selected when you
        let go, even an element only partly inside it. Hold
        <key>Shift</key> to add to the selection instead of replacing
        it.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Select All</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>A</key></keyseq>),
    <gui>Deselect All</gui>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>A</key></keyseq>) and
    <gui>Invert Selection</gui> are in the <gui>Edit</gui> menu.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="move">
    <title>Move</title>
    <p>Drag the selection to move it; several elements move together. The
    arrow keys move it by one dot, the finest step a printer can place, or
    by ten with <key>Shift</key> held. The arrows work while the canvas has
    the keyboard, which a click on it gives it.</p>
    <p>Nothing can be moved off the label: an element pushed against an edge
    stays against it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="resize">
    <title>Resize</title>
    <p>A single selected element has eight handles, on its corners and the
    middles of its sides. Drag one to resize the element.</p>
    <p>Some elements come back to the size they will really print at, rather
    than to the box you dragged:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p><em>Text</em> changes its font height and width, and its box
      is then the width the text prints.</p></item>
      <item><p>A <em>barcode</em> changes its bar height and the width of its
      narrowest bar.</p></item>
      <item><p>A <em>circle</em> stays round.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>A handle never makes a side smaller than 20 dots; type a smaller size
    in the Properties panel. <link xref="elements#handles"/> lists what each
    element's handles change, and <link xref="elements#snap"/> says
    why.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="group">
    <title>Group</title>
    <p>Select two or more elements and choose
    <guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Group</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>G</key></keyseq>). From then on a click on
    any of them selects them all, and they move, align and stack as one.
    Groups are saved in the label file, and a group can hold other
    groups.</p>
    <list>
      <item><p><keyseq><key>Ctrl</key></keyseq>-click one element of a group
      to select it alone, to move or change it without ungrouping.</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Remove from Group</gui> takes the element you
      <key>Ctrl</key>-clicked out of its group.</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Ungroup</gui>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>G</key></keyseq>) undoes
      the outermost group, one level at a time.</p></item>
      <item><p>A selected group has handles of its own. Dragging one scales
      every element in the group.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="align">
    <title>Align</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Align</gui></guiseq>, or the
    <gui>Align ▾</gui> button at the right of the menu bar, lines up the
    selection's left edges, centres, right edges, tops, middles or bottoms.
    With several things selected they line up with each other; with one
    element or one group selected it lines up with the label, so
    <gui>Centre Horizontally</gui> centres it across the label. A group
    moves as one box.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="order">
    <title>Put one element above another</title>
    <p>Elements are stacked in the order they were added, the newest on top.
    <gui>Bring to Front</gui>, <gui>Bring Forward</gui>,
    <gui>Send Backward</gui> and <gui>Send to Back</gui>, in the
    <gui>Edit</gui> menu and the right-click menu, move the element you
    clicked last. Stacking decides what a click picks, and what an element
    with <gui>Reverse</gui> ticked turns white, since it reverses what is
    already printed beneath it. It hides nothing on paper: the printer
    prints every element.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="copy">
    <title>Copy, paste and duplicate</title>
    <p><gui>Cut</gui>, <gui>Copy</gui> and <gui>Paste</gui>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>X</key></keyseq>,
    <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>C</key></keyseq>,
    <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>V</key></keyseq>) use the desktop clipboard,
    which holds the elements as ZPL. A paste lands where the copy came from,
    or a tenth of an inch down and to the right if something identical is
    already there. Paste also takes ZPL copied from anywhere else.
    <gui>Duplicate</gui> copies the selection without using the clipboard,
    and puts the copy a tenth of an inch down and to the right, or further
    while that spot is taken.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="print-this">
    <title>Keep an element off the printed label</title>
    <p>Right-click an element and clear <gui>Print This Element</gui>. It
    stays in the design and in the saved file, drawn faintly, but is not
    printed. This is how to keep text from printing through an image laid
    over it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="undo">
    <title>Undo and delete</title>
    <p><gui>Undo</gui> (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Z</key></keyseq>) and
    <gui>Redo</gui> (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>Z</key></keyseq>
    or <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Y</key></keyseq>) go back and forward up
    to 50 changes, and are also the arrows at the right of the menu bar.
    Opening a file starts the history afresh. <key>Delete</key> removes
    everything selected.</p>
  </section>
</page>
